---
import LearnArticle from "../../../components/LearnArticle.astro";
import type { FaqItem } from "../../../components/FAQ.astro";

const intro =
  "Every lottery ticket is one combination — one specific set of numbers out of every set the game allows. Counting those sets is what combinations are, and it's the single idea underneath all lottery odds.";

const faqs: FaqItem[] = [
  {
    question: "What is a combination in the lottery?",
    answer:
      "One combination is one complete set of numbers on a ticket — for Powerball, a specific 5 white balls plus a Powerball. The order of the white balls doesn't matter; {3, 11, 24, 35, 68} + 14 is the same combination however you write it.",
  },
  {
    question: "How many Powerball combinations are there?",
    answer:
      "292,201,338. There are 11,238,513 ways to choose 5 white balls from 69, times 26 possible Powerballs. Mega Millions has 290,472,336: 12,103,014 ways to choose 5 from 70, times 24 Mega Balls.",
  },
  {
    question: "How much would it cost to buy every combination?",
    answer:
      "At $2 per Powerball ticket, full coverage of all 292,201,338 combinations would cost about $584 million — more than most jackpots after tax, before even considering the logistics and the risk of splitting the prize. That's why 'buy every ticket' schemes never work.",
  },
  {
    question: "Does the order of numbers on my ticket matter?",
    answer:
      "No. Lottery combinations are unordered — matching the drawn numbers in any order wins. That's also why our number generator sorts the white balls on each ticket: {5, 12, 30} is the same ticket as {30, 5, 12}.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can two tickets have the same combination?",
    answer:
      "Yes, unless you check. Nothing stops two quick-picks from producing identical numbers, and duplicates waste money — you're paying twice for the same chance. Our generator deduplicates combinations by default for exactly this reason.",
  },
];

const sourceIds = [
  "powerball-official-rules",
  "megamillions-official-rules",
];
---

<LearnArticle
  slug="/learn/how-lottery-combinations-work/"
  intro={intro}
  faqs={faqs}
  sourceIds={sourceIds}
>
  <h2 class="pt-2 text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">Counting without listing</h2>
  <p>
    You can't write out 292 million tickets, but you can count them. The
    counting tool is the combination formula: the number of ways to choose{" "}
    <em>k</em> balls from <em>n</em>, where order doesn't matter. For
    Powerball's white balls that's "69 choose 5" = 11,238,513. Multiply by the
    26 possible Powerballs and you get the famous total: 292,201,338 possible
    tickets. Our{" "}
    <a href="/lottery-combination-generator/" class="font-semibold text-blue-700 hover:underline">
      combinations calculator
    </a>{" "}
    runs this for any game configuration — including custom ones you define
    yourself.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-2 text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">Why order doesn't matter</h2>
  <p>
    If order mattered, there would be far more "tickets" — 69 × 68 × 67 × 66
    × 65 arrangements of white balls instead of 11,238,513 combinations. But
    the lottery only cares which numbers were drawn, not the sequence, so
    every ordering of the same five numbers is one combination. This is also
    why generated tickets show sorted white balls: sorting is just tidiness,
    not strategy.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-2 text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">The "buy them all" fantasy</h2>
  <p>
    If every combination costs $2 and there are 292,201,338 of them, full
    coverage costs roughly $584 million — and you'd need to physically buy,
    store, and check 292 million tickets before the drawing, while risking
    that another winner splits the jackpot with you. The expected cost of
    covering all combinations always exceeds the prize; lotteries are
    designed that way. Smaller versions of the same idea (wheeling systems,
    syndicates buying thousands of tickets) improve the chance of{" "}
    <em>a</em> win while leaving the expected value negative.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-2 text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">Combinations vs. your ticket</h2>
  <p>
    Your ticket is one combination. A second, different ticket is a second
    combination. Ten different tickets are ten combinations out of 292
    million — which is exactly why each ticket's chance stays 1 in 292
    million no matter how many you buy. The{" "}
    <a href="/lottery-number-generator/" class="font-semibold text-blue-700 hover:underline">
      number generator
    </a>{" "}
    produces unique random combinations (deduplicated by default, up to 100
    sets), and the{" "}
    <a href="/lottery-probability-calculator/" class="font-semibold text-blue-700 hover:underline">
      probability explorer
    </a>{" "}
    translates combination counts into plain percentages.
  </p>
</LearnArticle>
